// Setup: N validators with separate DagState instances for each honest node
// to simulate independent views due to selective broadcast

// Round R1: Byzantine equivocation
// - Create two blocks (B_R1_a, B_R1_b) at same slot with different content
// - Selectively broadcast to partition honest nodes into groups

// Round R2: Honest divergence
// - Each honest node builds on the equivocating block it saw
// - This creates structurally different DAG branches

// Round R3: Cascading equivocation
// - Byzantine creates more equivocating blocks (B_R3_a, B_R3_b)
// - Each references ancestors from different DAG branches
// - Include different commit votes to amplify divergence

// Rounds R4+: Continue building
// - Build sufficient rounds to trigger commit logic
// - Each honest node builds on its divergent DAG view

// Commit and Linearize:
// - Run UniversalCommitter on each honest node's DagState
// - Run Linearizer on committed blocks
// - Collect linearized sequences

// Assert: All honest nodes must produce identical sequences
// - Same length
// - Same blocks at same positions
// - Same order
// If assertion fails: SAFETY VIOLATION detected
```

**Adaptation to Other Protocols**: This pattern can be tested in any DAG-based or blockchain consensus protocol by:
1. Simulating selective broadcast (separate state per node)
2. Creating multi-round equivocations with different ancestors
3. Allowing honest nodes to build on divergent views
4. Checking if commit/finalization produces identical results across all honest nodes
